        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Numerous cell science biotechnology investigations are conducted on Space Shuttle missions.
During in-flight biotechnology experiments, media solution from a concentrated liquid media
must be mixed with ultra-pure water from the surface to support tissue growth. Presently, the Biotechnology Program must launch all water required for experiment use during a mission. This adds significant mass and volume to each payload and represents a significant logistics penalty.
<p>
The purpose of the Biotechnology Water Treatment System (BWTS) is to produce ultra-pure
water from the Shuttle's fuel cell water. This water, when processed by BWTS, can replace
manifested ultra-pure water supplies, and thus significantly decrease the mass and volume
required to support Biotechnology payloads.
<p>
The objectives of this experiment were:<br>
I. To evaluate the performance of the BWTS in a microgravity environment.<br>
2. To evaluate the set-up and disassemble/stow procedure as well as the in-flight nominal
operation procedures.<br>
3. To evaluate the ability of the BWTS to produce ultra-pure grade water to be used in the Bioreactor Cell Science Experiments for support of cell and tissue growth.<br>
4. To return water generated by the BWTS, in a microgravity environment for use in ground analysis science verification tests.<br>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Pre-flight: The crewmembers received a familiarization session and two training sessions with a laboratory version of the BWTS within the Shuttle middeck training facility containing the galley.
<p>
In-flight: The BWTS and stowage items were removed. The BWTS QD is attached to the Shuttle galley auxiliary port using aseptic techniques. The operator did not leave the system during the time of water processing and collection in the sample bags. Water from the galley was processed through the BWTS and samples collected in sample.bags from from the BWTS outlet port.
Both start and stop collection times were recorded on each bag. After 11 samples were collected, one sample was collected at the BWTS port. Following collection of the influent water sample, the BWTS, sample bags, and all remaining items were stowed for return.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This was a hardware evaluation study. Results aren’t available.
<p>
